/** @page pgsqlreleasenotesguide Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    @section pgsql35 pgsql Driver Version 3.5
    - the \c "timezone" option now also sets the session's time zone on the server, when the connection is
      opened (including every connection in a \c DatasourcePool), when it is reestablished after it was lost,
      and when the option is changed on an open connection; previously the option only told the driver which
      zone the server used, so a connection whose option did not match the server's zone stored and returned
      plain \c timestamp values shifted by the difference.  Such connections now get consistent timestamp data,
      and server-side date/time functions (\c now()::timestamp, \c current_date, \c date_trunc(),
      \c to_char(), and column defaults such as <tt>DEFAULT now()</tt>) now use the declared zone on them.
      Connections without the option are unchanged
    - UTC offsets in the \c "timezone" option (ex: \c "+01:00") work; they are set on the server as offsets
      east of UTC, as in %Qore.  An invalid zone raises a \c DBI:PGSQL:TIMEZONE-ERROR exception and the
      connection is not opened
    - a connection reestablished after it was lost keeps the datasource's character encoding; previously it
      used the database's default encoding, so strings were sent and read in the wrong encoding after the
      reconnect when the datasource's encoding differed from the database's
    - typed timestamp and time binds with precision retain their WITH TIME ZONE qualifier,
      preserving offsets in both scalar and array values

    - public API documentation includes generated QPP declarations and binding links
    - CMake uninstall honors staging paths and preserves unrelated files and directories

    - fixed pgvector test setup on fresh databases by installing the extension before
      opening the connection that discovers and tests its types
    - added the settable \c "tcp-user-timeout" connection option (libpq \c tcp_user_timeout), enabled
      by default with a 30-second limit; a query in flight when the server's host disappears without
      closing the connection now fails once its data has gone unacknowledged for that long, instead
      of blocking for about 15 minutes until the kernel's retransmission limit; keepalives do not
      cover this case because they only probe idle connections
      (see @ref pgsql_connection_options)
    - fixed binding an argument that holds a weak (\c ":=") or opaque (\c "@=") reference: a
      prepared statement walks the argument list it was given, so such an element is what the
      iterator yields rather than its target; a weak reference failed with
      <tt>don't know how to bind type 'weak list reference'</tt> and an opaque one crashed the
      driver, even though the target binds normally
    - fixed a heap buffer overflow binding a \c number with more than ~197 decimal digits; the
      binary \c numeric encoder wrote its base-10000 digits into a fixed 50-element array with no
      bound check, which corrupted the heap and made the server reject the value with
      <tt>invalid digit in external "numeric" value</tt>.  %Qore raises a number's working
      precision on every addition to keep sums exact, so a quotient of accumulated sums reaches
      that length without anything unusual happening in the calling code
    - fixed the display scale sent with a bound \c number: it accumulated the number of
      \a remaining fraction digits once per four-digit group instead of the fraction length, so a
      value with more than four decimal places was stored with trailing zeros
      (\c 0.12345 became \c 0.123450), and a long fraction could also overflow the server's
      maximum scale
    - added \c DBI_CAP_HAS_BULK_LOAD support using a persistent PostgreSQL
      <tt>COPY ... FROM STDIN</tt> session; BulkSqlUtil can opt in with \c "bulk_load": \c True or
      use \c "auto" policy, and each buffered row block is sent with one \c PQputCopyData() call
    - native COPY owns bounded stream reporting, so BulkSqlUtil does not double-report events; byte
      accounting is the exact escaped text-COPY payload sent to libpq, including separators and row
      terminators
    - native COPY validates row-block shapes, checks cooperative cancellation while rendering large
      blocks, aborts cleanly on observer refusal or overrun, and drains the server result on every end
      path so the connection can be reused after rollback
    - the raw <tt>COPY ... FROM STDIN</tt> SQL detection path remains supported unchanged
    - removed the autotools build; the module is now built with CMake only

    @section pgsql34 pgsql Driver Version 3.4
    - \c COPY \c FROM \c STDIN operations now report bounded stream boundaries to a datasource
      mutation observer when the driver is built against %Qore 3.0 or later; declared and consumed
      byte counts are reported without the payload ever being buffered, and a consumer can reject a
      stream before any data is sent or stop it while it is streaming
      (see @ref pgsqlmutationobserverguide)
    - added settable libpq connection options (\c "keepalives", \c "keepalives-idle",
      \c "keepalives-interval", \c "keepalives-count", and \c "connect-timeout"); TCP keepalives are
      now enabled by default with a 60-second idle time so the server promptly reaps backends orphaned
      by an unclean client exit instead of leaving them idle until the OS keepalive default (often 2
      hours), which could exhaust \c max_connections (see @ref pgsql_connection_options)
    - added the settable \c "application-name" connection option, which reports a libpq
      \c application_name to the server (visible in \c pg_stat_activity.application_name) so the client
      or connection pool owning each backend can be identified (see @ref pgsql_connection_options)
    - added pgvector extension type support (\c vector, \c halfvec, \c sparsevec): type OIDs are
      automatically discovered at connection time by querying \c pg_type; the module works
      transparently on databases with or without pgvector installed
      (<a href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector">pgvector</a>)
    - added pgsql_bind_vector() function for convenient vector binding
    - vectors are returned as typed \c list<float> values; halfvec values are decoded from
      IEEE 754 half-precision; sparsevec values are returned as hashes with \c dim, \c nnz,
      \c indices, and \c values keys
    - added support for \c vector[] array binds via string type name mechanism
    - added \c vector, \c halfvec, and \c sparsevec to the \c describe() output with
      appropriate Qore type mappings
    - fixed a pre-existing memory leak in text-format typed scalar binds where the string buffer
      was not freed in \c reset() (only \c TEXTOID was handled; now all text-format non-array binds
      are freed)
    - fixed a pre-existing uninitialized memory read in \c reset() by value-initializing \c parambuf
    - added columnar DBI result support with \c Datasource::selectColumnar() and
      \c SQLStatement::fetchColumnar() when built against %Qore with columnar DBI APIs, including dense buffers
      for PostgreSQL \c boolean, integer, floating-point, string-like, bounded \c numeric(p,s), date/time, and
      interval result columns where the linked %Qore runtime supports the corresponding dense logical column type

    @section pgsql33 pgsql Driver Version 3.3
    - added \c DBI_CAP_HAS_ARRAY_BIND support: Qore lists are automatically encoded as PostgreSQL
      binary arrays, enabling efficient bulk DML via the \c BulkSqlUtil module and PostgreSQL's
      \c unnest() function; supported types: \c int, \c float, \c number, \c string, \c bool,
      \c date, and \c binary with NULL support within arrays
    - added \c COPY \c FROM \c STDIN protocol support for maximum-throughput bulk inserts: when a
      \c COPY statement is executed with a hash-of-lists argument, the driver streams data directly
      to PostgreSQL using the binary COPY protocol
    - added array bind size validation: all list parameters bound in the same statement must have
      the same number of elements
    - added native UUID type support with pgsql_bind_uuid() function
    - added schema introspection SQL constants (PGSQL_SQL_LIST_TABLES, PGSQL_SQL_TABLE_COLUMNS, etc.)
    - added additional character encoding mappings (Windows code pages 1250-1258)
    - fixed a memory leak when \c add() failed mid-parse (e.g., array size mismatch): extra
      \c parambuf entries beyond \c nParams were not freed in \c reset()
    - fixed a bug retrieving negative \c INT2 and \c INT4 values (were returned as unsigned)
    - fixed a bug in interval array binding where hours were incorrectly calculated
    - fixed a bug in boolean array binding where \c htonl() was incorrectly applied
    - fixed server description format in error messages

    @section pgsql321 pgsql Driver Version 3.2.1
    - fixed support for PostgreSQL 18
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5002">issue 5002</a>)

